# Why do the opening rules exist?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | calm, warm chess coach","b":"gemini:Puck | eager club player, relaxed and good-humoured; never exaggerated"}
prompt: "What do tempo, the centre and king safety buy you? When is it right to break an opening rule?"
next: {"title":"How do you learn from your own games?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/chess-30-days/06-how-do-you-learn-from-your-own-games.cast"}

## Warm-up: captures first
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r1bqk2r/ppp2ppp/2np4/2b1p2n/2B1P3/2NP4/PPP2PPP/R1BQK1NR w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [Qxh5]}
      - {fen: 2r3k1/pp3ppp/3p1b2/4n3/3P4/4BN2/PP3PPP/4R1K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [dxe5, dxe5]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title Warm-up

A: Today: why the opening rules exist, and when to break them. First, two warm-up puzzles from week one.
    draw position

A: White to move, still in the opening.
    highlight piece_c3 piece_c4

B: I'd bring the knight out. Develop first, the rules say.
    highlight piece_g1

A: Rules come second. Ask the Three Questions first: any captures?
    highlight piece_d1

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Captures first: which black piece has no guard?","Your queen's diagonal."] themes ["hanging piece"] voice a
        right: Queen takes h5: the knight on the rim had no defender.
        wrong: Look at every capture before any developing move.

B: A free knight. I'd have developed right past it.
    highlight piece_h5

A: Second one. Black's knight on e5 is attacked twice and defended twice.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: Then knight takes, and we just trade.
    highlight piece_f3

A: Count the values, not just the numbers. Which attacker is the cheapest?
    highlight piece_d4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Send the cheapest attacker in first.","Your d-pawn."] themes ["counting"] voice a
        right: Pawn takes the knight, and a pawn takes back: a pawn for a knight.
        wrong: Two against two: which of your attackers costs the least?

A: The pawn goes first, and a knight is yours. Keep that habit: the Three Questions before any rule.
    point at.ref sq_e5 gesture circle

## What the rules buy
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [e4, e5, Qh5, Nc6, Bc4, g6, Qf3, Nf6, Qb3, Nd4, Bxf7+, Ke7]}
      - {fen: 7k/8/8/8/8/5N2/8/K7 w - - 0 1, highlights: [d2, d4, e1, e5, g1, g5, h2, h4]}
      - {fen: 7k/8/8/8/8/7N/8/K7 w - - 0 1, highlights: [f2, f4, g1, g5]}
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nf6, Nxe5, Nxe4, Qe2, Nf6, Nc6+, Be7, Nxd8]}
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [e4, Nf6, e5, Nd5, c4, Nb6, d4, d6, f4], arrows: [{from: d6, to: e5}, {from: b6, to: c4}]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
sources:
    - id: alekhine
      title: Alekhine's Defence
      authors: Wikipedia contributors; Edward Winter (Chess Notes)
      year: 2026
      finding: 1.e4 Nf6 is named after Alexander Alekhine, who introduced it at the Budapest tournament of 1921 (against Endre Steiner and Fritz Sämisch); a hypermodern defence that lets White build a pawn centre in order to attack it.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alekhine%27s_Defence
    - id: petrov
      title: Petrov's Defence
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: After 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nf6 3.Nxe5, 3...Nxe4? is a mistake because of 4.Qe2; then 4...Nf6?? loses the queen to 5.Nc6+, a discovered check.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petrov%27s_Defence

    card title "What the rules buy"

A: Opening rules aren't manners. Each one buys you something: time, the centre, or a safe king.
    draw position

B: I like to get my queen out early. Queen to h5 hits e5 and f7 at once.
    animate plies_shown 3 duration 2.6

A: And Black meets each threat and gains time. Your queen moves again; Black develops.
    animate plies_shown 8 duration 5

B: Fine. Queen to b3, and f7 again.
    animate plies_shown 9 duration 1.3

A: Knight to d4 hits the queen and c2. She's chased again.
    animate plies_shown 10 duration 1.3

B: Can't I just take on f7, with check?
    animate plies_shown 11 duration 1.3

A: The king walks to e7. Your queen is still hit, and the bishop can't be saved too.
    animate plies_shown 12 duration 1.3

A: Count it: three of White's five moves were queen moves.
    animate plies_shown 10 duration 1.6

A: Black has two knights in play.
    highlight piece_d4 piece_f6

A: That's a tempo: one move's worth of time. Spend three on one piece, and the others stay home.
    highlight piece_b1 piece_c1 piece_g1

A: The centre buys reach.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: A knight on f3 touches eight squares.
    draw hl_d2 hl_d4 hl_e1 hl_e5 hl_g1 hl_g5 hl_h2 hl_h4 parallel true

B: A knight's a knight, wherever it stands.
    highlight piece_f3

    erase hl_d2 hl_d4 hl_e1 hl_e5 hl_g1 hl_g5 hl_h2 hl_h4

    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    quiz "The same knight, on h3. How many squares does it reach?" on_canvas true voice a
        right: Four: g1, f2, f4 and g5. Its other four jumps land off the board.
        wrong: Count its jumps; some land off the board.
        * 8
        + 4
        * 2

A: Four. Half its power, just from standing at the edge.
    draw hl_f2 hl_f4 hl_g1 hl_g5 parallel true

    erase hl_f2 hl_f4 hl_g1 hl_g5

A: Now the king.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Black answers e4 with e5 and knight f6, and White takes on e5.
    animate plies_shown 5 duration 4

B: Easy. I take his pawn back, just like he took mine.
    animate plies_shown 6 duration 1.3

A: Queen to e2 hits your knight, and looks straight down the open file at your king.
    animate plies_shown 7 duration 1.3

B: Then my knight goes back home to f6.
    animate plies_shown 8 duration 1.3

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Your knight on e5 is all that stands between your queen and the king.","A knight move with check."] themes ["discovered attack","opening"] voice a
        right: Knight to c6: check from White's queen. The bishop blocks, and the knight takes the queen on d8.
        wrong: Move the knight off the e-file, and hit something as it goes.

A: Black had better moves there.
    animate plies_shown 7 duration 2.5

A: But the natural retreat opened the file, and the uncastled king cost the queen.
    animate plies_shown 8 duration 1.3

B: Hold on. White's queen came out on move four. You said don't.
    highlight piece_e2

A: Because it hit something.
    animate plies_shown 7 duration 1.3

A: A rule gives way to a real threat: that's why the Three Questions come first.
    highlight piece_e4

A: Strong players break rules on purpose too.
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Alexander Alekhine, for one: in 1921 he answered e4 with a knight, and kept moving it.
    portrait alekhine cites ["alekhine"] x 88 y 540 width 130 of "Alexander Alekhine"

A: He paid in tempo to lure White's pawns forward,
    animate plies_shown 9 duration 3.4

A: where they could be attacked later.
    draw arrow_0 arrow_1 parallel true

    erase alekhine

A: So each rule is a price: a tempo, a square, a safe king.
    highlight piece_b6

A: Break one only when the board pays you back.
    highlight piece_c4 piece_d4 piece_e5 piece_f4

## Your turn: punish the opening
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Four for four. Your opponents should castle sooner.
    good: Most of them. The rest will turn up in your games.
    poor: Openings punish fast. Run the Three Questions and try again.
    none: The puzzles will wait for you.
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r1bqkb1r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/4p2Q/2B1P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K1NR w KQkq - 4 4, moves: [Qxf7#]}
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/6P1/5P2/PPPPP2P/RNBQKBNR b KQkq - 0 2, moves: [Qh4#], flip: true}
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/p3pppp/8/1p6/2pP4/4P3/1P3PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 6, moves: [Qf3, Nc6, Qxc6+]}
      - {fen: r1bqkb1r/pppn1ppp/5n2/3N2B1/3P4/8/PP2PPPP/R2QKBNR b KQkq - 0 6, moves: [Nxd5, Bxd8, Bb4+, Qd2, Bxd2+, Kxd2, Kxd8], flip: true}
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, Bc4]}
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, d4]}
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, Nc3, Nf6]}
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [d4, d5, c4, e6, Nc3, Nf6]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
    openings:
      - {name: Italian Game, eco: C50, side: white, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, Bc4], idea: 'The bishop aims at f7, and White is one move from castling.'}
      - {name: Scotch Game, eco: C45, side: white, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, d4], idea: A second pawn strikes the centre at once.}
      - {name: Four Knights Game, eco: C47, side: white, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, Nc3, Nf6], idea: Both sides bring their knights toward the centre before anything else.}
      - {name: Queen's Gambit Declined, eco: D37, side: white, moves: [d4, d5, c4, e6, Nc3, Nf6], idea: White offers a wing pawn for the centre; Black declines and develops.}
sources:
    - id: fool
      title: Fool's mate
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: Fool's mate (1.f3 e5 2.g4 Qh4#) is the fastest possible checkmate from the starting position.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fool%27s_mate

    card title "Your turn"

A: Now that you know what the rules buy, punish the players who skipped them.
    draw position

A: Four puzzles, then the openings themselves.
    highlight piece_f7

    pause 0.3

    ask "Mate in one." widget chess line true hints ["Checks first.","What guards f7?"] themes ["opening","mating pattern"] voice a
        right: Queen takes f7, mate. The bishop guards the queen.
        wrong: Which square near Black's king does only the king defend?

B: That's my queen trick. It only works if Black ignores f7.
    highlight piece_f6

A: Now Black's side: the board turns.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: White has pushed the two pawns beside his king.
    highlight piece_f3 piece_g4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Mate in one." widget chess line true hints ["Checks first.","The diagonal from your queen to White's king."] themes ["opening","king attack"] voice a
        right: Queen to h4, mate. Nothing can block, and the king can't move.
        wrong: Which diagonal leads straight to White's king now?

A: The fastest mate in chess: two moves, and the king never had a guard.
    highlight piece_e1

A: Back to White. Next, greed.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Black grabbed a pawn and spent his moves holding on to it.
    highlight piece_c4 piece_b5

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Black's rook still stands on a8.","A queen move down the long diagonal."] themes ["opening","threat"] voice a
        right: Queen to f3 hits the rook on a8. The knight blocks on c6, and the queen takes it with check.
        wrong: Find the black piece that never moved, then a queen move that hits it.

B: The rook in the corner never moved, and the knight that blocked fell.
    highlight piece_a8

A: And one from week one, from Black's side again.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: White has grabbed a pawn on d5, sure your knight can't move.
    highlight piece_f6

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line 3 hints ["Count to the end: what if the knight moves anyway?","After Bxd8, a bishop check."] themes ["counting","opening"] voice a
        right: Knight takes d5. White takes your queen, and bishop to b4, check, wins White's queen back.
        wrong: Let the knight take, then count every capture after it, checks included.

A: Count to the end: you give the queen, and get it back with a knight on top.
    animate plies_shown 7 duration 4.5

A: Now four openings, a few moves each: the Italian,
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 5 duration 0.4

A: the Scotch,
    animate position_shown 5 plies_shown 5 duration 0.4

A: the Four Knights,
    animate position_shown 6 plies_shown 6 duration 0.4

A: and the Queen's Gambit.
    animate position_shown 7 plies_shown 6 duration 0.4

A: Play them from memory.
    explore activity openings_drill

B: Every move is a centre pawn or a new piece.
    highlight piece_d4 piece_c4 piece_c3 piece_e6 piece_f6

A: So the rules are the price of time, the centre and a safe king.
    highlight piece_d4 piece_c4

A: Pay them, unless the Three Questions find you something better.
    highlight piece_c3

    erase position

A: Homework: one slow game today, fifteen plus ten or slower.
    text hw "15 + 10" x 500 y 450 font_size 72

A: Then find its first mistake.
    text hw2 "1st mistake?" x 500 y 330 font_size 56

